Q: Shift array elements A: <p>Consider this solution. Shifts elements by k.</p>
<pre><code>int* shift(int n, int *arr1, int k) {
    int *arr2 = new int[n];
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) {
        int index = (i - k + n) % n;
        arr2[i] = arr1[index];
    }
    return arr2;
}

</code></pre>
<p>Change the array style to an array style of your choice.</p>

Q: How to get real time of a midi event A: <p>You can use my <a href="https://github.com/melanchall/drywetmidi" rel="nofollow noreferrer">DryWetMIDI</a> library which does all these calculations for you:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>var midiFile = MidiFile.Read(&quot;Jenkins.mid&quot;);
var tempoMap = midiFile.GetTempoMap();

foreach (var trackChunk in midiFile.GetTrackChunks())
{
    foreach (var timedEvent in trackChunk.GetTimedEvents())
    {
        if (timedEvent.Event.EventType != MidiEventType.NoteOn)
           continue;

        MetricTimeSpan metricTime = timedEvent.TimeAs&lt;MetricTimeSpan&gt;(tempoMap);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Here we get metric time (hours/minutes/seconds/ms), but the library provides several other formats you can convert MIDI time to. Please read the article of the library docs to learn more: <a href="https://melanchall.github.io/drywetmidi/articles/high-level-managing/Time-and-length.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Time and length</a>.</p>
<p>More than that if you actually want to get notes instead of events, it's super easy with DryWetMIDI:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>var notes = midiFile.GetNotes();
</code></pre>
<p>You can also use <code>TimeAs</code> method on notes.</p>
<p>Also please read <a href="https://melanchall.github.io/drywetmidi/articles/high-level-managing/Getting-objects.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Getting objects</a> article to learn about more what kinds of objects (and their combinations) you can collect.</p>
